850:🔧 **F-2321-1 — THE CROSS-ENGINE GUARDS' DECISION MODULE OWNED THE VERDICT BUT NOT THE INPUT IT IS COMPUTED FROM, SO ITS BOTH-DIRECTIONS GUARD WAS BLIND TO THE ARGUMENT (fire s2321 2026-08-26).** `scripts/cross-engine-skip.mjs` exists to own one question — *may the cross-engine determinism guard run HERE?* — and `cross-engine-skip.test.mjs` pins BOTH directions of it, explicitly so nobody can "simplify" it into an unconditional skip. But `crossEngineSkipReason(env, interpreterCount)` takes the count as an **ARGUMENT**, and both consumers computed it independently from their own verbatim copy of the candidate list (`gr-sim.test.mjs`, `wave-scaling-cross-engine.test.mjs`, identical two-entry arrays `.filter(existsSync)`). **That is F-2209-1's rule one level out: extracting a decision to make it testable creates a new untested seam, and here the seam is not the call site but the argument.** The guard fabricates its counts, so it is structurally incapable of seeing the two consumers drift — or of seeing an nvm upgrade leave both with a single engine, which SKIPS, which keeps every battery in the repo green while the cross-engine determinism property stops being tested at all. ⚖️ **SEVERITY STATED HONESTLY AND DELIBERATELY NOT INFLATED: LATENT.** `~/.nvm/versions/node/v23.11.1/bin/node` is present (11 engines installed), so both consumers resolve 2 engines today and every green this streak has read was TRUE — verified, not assumed. The defect is that neither could tell you when that stopped being true, and that the fix required two edits nothing checked. 🔑 **THE CORPUS ALREADY HELD THE PATTERN, for the fourth fire running: `assay-replay-agent.mjs` exports `CANONICAL_ASSAY_NODE_VERSION` and `assay-worker.test.mjs` imports it — one owner, one constant. These two files had simply never adopted it.** 🛠️ **CURE (`ca7f9ef2d`): `NODE_ENGINE_CANDIDATES` + `installedNodeEngines(candidates = …)` move into `cross-engine-skip.mjs`, beside the decision they feed. `candidates` is a PARAMETER for the same reason `crossEngineSkipReason` takes `env`: the existence FILTER is the load-bearing behaviour and on a box where every candidate is installed there is no other way to exercise it — an arm no defect can reach is decoration (s2226).** 🚫 **THE LIST IS STILL WRITTEN DOWN RATHER THAN GLOBBED, and that restraint is measured, not lazy: these guards assert determinism ACROSS TWO NAMED ENGINES, so which two is part of the claim. What is cured is the DUPLICATION, not the pinning.** 🚦 **AND `gr-sim.test.mjs` DELIBERATELY DID NOT ADOPT `crossEngineSkipReason`'s FIRE-SHELL ARM — the restraint is the point: that is an owner-priced ruling (F-1408-2/s1409) scoped to `wave-scaling-cross-engine` on ITS measured cost, and extending it to a different subject would silently delete this test's coverage from every fire. An over-general cure here is indistinguishable from a coverage deletion, and it would have been invisible.** 📊 **RE-MEASURED WHILE HERE, AND IT CONFIRMS RATHER THAN OVERTURNS F-2166-2 — banked because the law says these figures only ever drift and must be re-taken: the `overtime`-on-both-engines test costs `92,927 ms` of its 240 s budget (**38.7%**) at load avg 2.21, against s2166's `91,282 ms` (38.0%) and s2076's `219,418 ms` (**91.4%**) under load. So it is NOT about to red at rest; the margin is load-attributable exactly as F-2166-2 says, and the alarming reading of s2076 is refuted as a structural claim.** ✅ **BEHAVIOUR-NEUTRAL BEFORE LANDING (F-1274-2): resolved list byte-identical to the legacy expression, with the control asserting its own validity first (F-2215-1: "control arm produced 2 engines — it really ran"); the consuming test `92,927 ms PASS` → `90,716 ms PASS`; `cross-engine-skip.test.mjs` 3/3; `wave-scaling` 3 SKIP as before; tsc clean; `gate-caller-audit` PASS.** 🔒 **Guarded by `scripts/cross-engine-engine-source-guard.test.mjs` — 5 arms, 56 ms, rooted in `test:ledger-guards`.** 🔬 **TEETH, all six variants manufactured on scratch copies and every file verified byte-identical afterwards: PRE-CURE `gr-sim` restored verbatim reds 3 · PRE-CURE `wave-scaling` reds 3 · the PARTIAL cure (imports the module, keeps its own private list) reds the same 3, so a half-adoption cannot pass · resolver stops filtering reds 1 · resolver invents an engine reds 2 · candidate list collapsed to ONE engine reds 1. **Every one of the 5 arms reds in at least one variant, so none is decoration** — s2226's reachability duty, taken as a sweep rather than assumed.** ⚠️ **ONE PREDICTION CORRECTED: I expected the invented-engine variant to red the filter arm alone; it reds the candidate-list arm too. Benign over-coverage, recorded because this streak's running note is that the reverse controls keep repricing what their author was surest of.** 💡 **REUSABLE, and it is an AIMING rule: this streak has censused catch handlers, guard-keyed empties, cwd roots and linked-worktree staleness — every one of them asking how a corpus can come back EMPTY. This one comes back FULL and WRONG-BY-DUPLICATION, and no census on those keys can see it. When a module is extracted to own a decision, ask what its verdict is computed FROM, and whether that input is owned by the same module — a both-directions guard over fabricated inputs proves the decision and says nothing about the argument.** ⓘ Two negative results banked while aiming, so the next fire inherits reasons rather than re-taking them: **(1)** the ENV-VAR axis is near-empty — 42 of 333 non-test scripts read `process.env`, only `red-inventory-lookup.mjs` (`RED_INVENTORY_PATH`/`_COMPACT_PATH`), `run-guards.mjs` (`GR_GUARD_STATS_PATH`) and `twin-banks-stall-census.mjs` (`GR_TB_CENSUS_ROOT`) let an env var select a CORPUS, and `red-inventory-lookup` is already hardened (an explicit *"ABSENT and MALFORMED are different answers and must not share a branch"* comment, every read inside a try that `fail()`s at **exit 2**). **(2)** the ENV-KEYED SKIP axis is empty — exactly ONE conditional skip in the whole test corpus keys on `process.env` (`skillmd-guard.test.mjs:61`, a deliberate self-recursion guard for its own manufactured-defect child). The battery's 5 skips are fully accounted for: 3 cross-engine fire-shell (declared, with a reason naming F-1408-2) + 2 F-E2S-3 owner-ruled — corroborating s2166's `498 pass / 5 skipped` from a second direction.
