at made them de-listed in the first place. **This is the s2106 shape (F-1501-3 + F-1640-1, adjacent 454 handoffs and never once linked) at THREE rows instead of two.** ⚖️ **THE TWO GROUNDS, STATED HONESTLY SO THE OWNER IS NOT OVER-SOLD.** The live exemption reason holds the pair out on two independent terms: ⑴ **neither declares `twist.pressureEnabled`** — measured, that key appears exactly twice in the E2 contracts, `:134` (hill mine) and `:574` (pressure garden), so trestle and incline can build no boiler, burn no coal, and the E2 arsenal has nothing to spend; ⑵ **both field a railcar at `hpScale: 30`** against the hill mine's 12.5. **F-1608-2's cure moves term ⑵ ONLY, so ruling it alone would NOT re-admit either map** — which is precisely why the three collapse into one question rather than three independent ones, and why answering them separately would waste two of the owner's words. 🚫 **WHAT I DID NOT DO, EACH REFUSAL LOAD-BEARING. ⑴ I closed no desk item and the desk stays 29** — only the owner's word closes an owner-desk row (§7.3), and F-E2S-3's own text reserves that word. **⑵ I did not edit a line of code** — not the exemptions table, not the contracts, not a citation string; re-admitting or re-tuning a map is the owner's fork by all three rows' own gates. **⑶ I did not author a master** — the live question is a design fork, which §2E's HARD LIMIT forbids authoring against. **⑷ I did not re-run the arsenal battery**: the securing evidence above is **quoted** from the review, not re-measured by me. **What I measured first-hand is the DOOR** — and the door is the half that the stale sentence got wrong. 🔒 **HONEST LIMITS. ⑴** I did not verify that hill-mine still secures *today*; I verified that main **admits** it, which is the claim F-E2S-3 contradicts. **⑵** The 13-of-29 alarm census is keyed on `desk-state-audit`'s resolved line numbers, so a desk item whose row moved since that resolution would be miscounted; the three rows I acted on were each re-anchored and proved **present and unique** before use. **⑶** I did not audit the other 10 alarm-carrying rows — they are named below for the next fire, unswept. 💡 **THE REUSABLE HALF, AND IT SHARPENS s2107's RULE RATHER THAN REPEATING IT: THE ALARM PHRASE FINDS THE ROW, BUT THE ROT IS OFTEN NOT IN THE FIGURE THE ROW FLAGGED — IT IS IN THE ATTRIBUTION SITTING BESIDE IT.** F-1608-2 flagged a number and guarded it well; every figure it cites is intact to the coordinate. What decayed was the four words *"per F-E2S-3"* — a pointer to **why**, which no ratchet watches, which no figure sweep would have checked, and which was the thread that led to a refuted headline, a doubly-rotted verification and a discharged gate. **A row can be perfectly right about its numbers and stale about its reasons, and the reasons are what the owner actually rules on.** Corollary: **when a row cites another row as its reassurance, re-read the CITED row — the reassurance is only as live as the thing it points at.** Related: [F-1608-2], [F-E2S-3], [F-E2PA-6], [F-E2S-4], [F-1660-1], [F-2072-1], [F-2107-1], [F-1501-3], [F-1640-1], [F-DOOR-4].
1475:✅ **F-2099-1 — THE s2098 CURE IS VERIFIED IN SITU AND THE BOARD IS GREEN; THE LAW'S COST CITATION HAD DRIFTED 2.2× AGAIN; AND F-2076-1's RED IS LOAD-ATTRIBUTABLE, NOT STRUCTURAL (s2099 2026-08-21, dry board — MEASURED, ALL THREE IN ONE BATTERY RUN).** 📋 **WHY THIS RUN EXISTED AT ALL, because a dry board is not a reason to run an expensive battery — a gap in evidence is.** s2098 cured `scripts/claimed-spec-harness-guard.mjs` (the three `process.exit()` calls → `process.exitCode` + `return`, from a sampled V8 deadlock) and gated it on **its own 12-test suite plus `test:ledger-guards`**. But that guard is a LEAF of `test:node-guards`, and the hang it cures had only ever been observed **inside the full battery, under the battery's own arrangement** — so the one arrangement that had never been re-run post-cure was the only one where the defect had ever appeared. **A cure verified only in the suite it ships with is verified in the arrangement that never broke.** The law also requires this battery run ALONE, and an empty six-queue board with an idle runner is the one condition under which that is honestly possible (s1536 hung ~19 min proving the converse). ✅ **RESULT — GREEN, AND `cancelled 0` IS THE LOAD-BEARING COLUMN, NOT `fail 0`:** `rc=0`, **`472 tests / 467 pass / 0 fail / 0 cancelled / 5 skipped` in `404.7 s`**. F-2076-1 established that this battery's failures arrive as **`cancelled`** (a timeout) rather than `fail` (an assertion), so a fire reading only the `fail` column would have called s2076's red green too. **The harness guard ran inside the full battery and did not hang.** ⚖️ **STATED HONESTLY: this is ONE clean run, and s2098 itself could not reproduce the deadlock in 180 bounded attempts — so a single green cannot prove a rare race cured.** It removes the arrangement-shaped hole in the evidence; it does not close the race. The 5 skips are the documented F-E2S-3 Baron pins plus the cross-engine class, labelled in place. 📈 **SECOND FINDING, FOUND BY DOING THE FIRST — THE COST CITATION IN `scripts/fire.md:31` HAD DRIFTED 2.2× AND THE SCHEDULING ADVICE WAS THE FALSE PART.** It claimed s1537's `363 tests / 360 pass / 3 skipped` in `181.3 s` and told the next fire to budget **"3 minutes serial, not one"**. Measured tonight: **404.7 s — SEVEN minutes.** `gr-sim.test.mjs` is **still** dominant and has grown with the battery: **17 tests / 227.3 s / 56.2% of wall** (was 12 / 79.9 s / 44%); the runner-up is a SINGLE test, fixture-teardown's *"all 34 scripts/\*.test.mjs fixture owners remove their temp directories"*, at **69.6 s**; **14 tests over 5 s carry 313.5 s = 77% of wall**, so the cost is concentrated and a future trim has an obvious target. 🔑 **THE SEQUENCE IS THE FINDING, NOT THE NUMBER: 284 → 363 → 424 (s1613) → 472 tests, and 55.6 → 181.3 → 280.9 → 404.7 s.** This is the **third** correction of the same sentence (s1462, s1537, now), it has **only ever moved upward**, and no guard watches it — `law-pointer-guard` checks COORDINATES and this coordinate never moves, while the figures stay syntactically valid as they go quietly wrong. **A cost citation that only drifts upward under-budgets the duty by exactly the amount the factory has grown**, and under-budgeting is what makes a fire overlap this battery with something else — which is the s1536 contamination, i.e. the drift causes the defect the same clause warns about. ✅ Corrected in place at `scripts/fire.md:31` as a **SAME-LINE edit** (47 lines before and after, `law-pointer-guard` PASS: 7 surfaces / 29 pointers / 0 rotten); the s1462 and s1537 figures are **RESTATED, NOT DELETED**, per the Retention Law — they are the provenance of the drift. **This discharges the GATE F-1537-1 wrote for itself** (*"a fire with budget greps the law surfaces for measured figures and re-measures the ones a command can settle"*) for the largest such figure in the corpus. ⚠️ **THIRD FINDING, AND IT CORRECTS AN INHERITANCE THE VERY NEXT FIRE WOULD OTHERWISE HAVE MADE: F-2076-1's *"the battery reds in the fire shell"* IS TRUE-AS-MEASURED BUT MUST NOT BE CARRIED AS A STANDING PROPERTY — the margin moves with machine load.** All three tests it named as budget-endangered passed here, **one day later, same shell (node v26.4.0, `CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR` set, file concurrency 1), same declared budgets, with 2.0–2.4× more headroom**: `runtime rush and --overtime…` (90 s budget) **90,003 ms CANCELLED at s2076 → 46,297 ms PASS, 51.4%** · `overtime banks the Claim…on both Node engines` (240 s) **219,418 ms, 91.4% → 91,282 ms, 38.0%** · `Twin Banks consumes its declared crossings…` (45 s) **34,896 ms, 77.5% → 16,275 ms, 36.2%**. Load average during my run: **3.75**. 🔬 **F-2076-1's MECHANISM IS UNREFUTED AND REMAINS THE DURABLE HALF** — v26 consults per-test `{ timeout }` overrides, v23 bounds at FILE granularity and never consults them, so the lanes have never once enforced these budgets. What is now measured is only that **the margin is a property of the ARRANGEMENT, not of the sim or the engine alone**, exactly as F-2076-1 itself hinted when it called this *"outside the envelope the number was chosen for"*. ➡️ **CONSEQUENCE FOR THE NEXT READER: a red here is a question about the arrangement BEFORE it is a question about the sim — check the load and `sample` the pid before theorising, and never re-pin to make it go away (F-1441-3).** ⓘ **Note the three call sites moved `:340`→`:345`, `:264`→`:271`, `:512`→`:517` since s2076 — cite by CONTENT; the coordinates drift.** 🚫 **WHAT I DID NOT CLAIM. (1)** I did **not** run a controlled load experiment: I measured a quiet machine and a fast battery **in correlation**, and did not prove load is the cause — a competing explanation (some other change landing between s2076 and now) is unexcluded, and a fire with budget could settle it by re-running under deliberate load. **(2)** I did **not** re-run `gr-sim.test.mjs` alone, so its 227.3 s here is an **in-battery** attribution (17 timed tests matched to their owning file by