1519:🔬 **F-2120-1 (s2120 2026-08-21, DRY BOARD — THE BOARD WAS CALLED DRY FOR 33 MORE FIRES WHILE A CLASS-BLOCKING, FIRE-AUTHORABLE CURE SAT IN A `RECORDED-NOT-BUILT` ROW. F-2086-2 NAMED THIS EXACT CLASS AND ITS CURE SWEPT ONE ROW OF THE FIRST DENOMINATOR ONLY.)** 📋 **THE INHERITED CLAIM.** s2115–s2119 each handed forward **PIPELINE-DRY: lanes a, c, d**, two of them citing `authorable-candidates` at **0 fire-authorable**. I re-ran it and **confirmed the number exactly**: 12 planned leaves, 12 priced, 0 authorable (owner-gated 7, attended-owed 3, prose-only 1, needs-spec 1) — and every price it cites is dated **2026-08-11**, ten days stale. ⚠️ **THE NUMBER IS RIGHT AND THE CONCLUSION IS WRONG, FOR THE SECOND TIME, BY THE SAME MECHANISM.** **F-2086-2** (s2086) already measured this: the instrument’s denominator is **planned leaves**, and it closed with the reusable half — *“the desk, the findings rows and the specs are three further denominators”*. **Its cure took ONE row from the FIRST denominator (the desk, `F-E2S-4`) and the other two were never swept.** A later handoff even carried the lesson forward as an instruction — *“price a lane against those before declaring PIPELINE-DRY”* — and **33 consecutive fires then re-declared PIPELINE-DRY citing only the planned-leaves tool.** That is the F-2115-1 shape exactly: a row naming its own next command, which never ran. ✅ **WHAT THE SECOND DENOMINATOR ACTUALLY HELD.** Sweeping the **findings rows**, `F-2086-1` is a 🔺 row carrying an explicit **`RECORDED-NOT-BUILT`** clause that names its own cure verbatim — *“widening the harness needs either a `--contract` argument or a relaxed board filter; both edit an existing sanctioned script, which that task’s firewall forbade”* — under a standing **`GATE: an E3+ census slice is not authorable until the harness can select its contract.`** It is a **CLASS blocker in its own words** (*“every future E2–E10 census slice hits it”*) whose failure mode is the dangerous one (*“the harness **succeeds** and returns numbers about the wrong contracts”*). It is instrument-only, zero product change, reversible — **fire-authorable on every axis, and it sat unbuilt for 33 fires.** 🔎 **PREMISE RE-VERIFIED AT SOURCE, NOT INHERITED (the §0.4 duty):** the filter is live at `scripts/gr-sim-campaign.mjs:67-68` (`the-claim || e1-*`); the parser at `:186` still accepts only `player/output/checkpoint/resume/test-fixture`; and `git log` on that file shows **exactly one commit ever** (`9849623b6`, the original E1 build) — so nothing has touched it since the finding. Mistake #8 checked: no master targets the cure, no done-move matches. 🎯 **AND THE FINDING’S OWN CURE SKETCH IS NECESSARY BUT NOT SUFFICIENT — FOUND BY READING, AND FOLDED INTO THE MASTER SO IT CANNOT BURN THE RUN. Three gates stand between the filter and an E3 number; F-2086-1 named only the first.** (1) the board filter `:67-68`; (2) the **unlock gate** `:73` → `contractUnlockStatus`, which returns `unlocked:false` for any inactive epoch (`src/meta/ContractUnlock.ts:22`), and `epochIsActive` is `active.order >= requested.order` (`src/meta/ContractFamilies.ts:1111-1115`) with a fresh profile at epoch-1 — so **`e3-canyon-works` is LOCKED by construction**; (3) the **campaign-walk loop** `:71-74`, a `while(true)` that `break`s when no candidate is unlocked, so a widened filter alone **exits having measured nothing** — the same silent-wrong-answer failure, merely relocated. The bench-seed constraint at `:78-79` is **not** a blocker: `e3-canyon-works` is seeded with both seeds (34 contracts seeded, all four E3s). ➡️ **DISPATCHED** as `tasks/f2120-1-campaign-harness-contract-select.md` (**lane-a**, leaf `f2120-1-campaign-harness-contract-select`), instrument-only: firewalled off `src/**`, `assets/contracts/**` and **any unlock bypass**, with the three refusal arms (unknown / unseeded / locked) required to exit **non-zero before any sim starts** and the locked arm required to print `contractUnlockStatus`’s own `condition` text. Backward compatibility is held by an **existing** control the master forbids editing — `scripts/gr-sim-campaign.test.mjs:62` (“campaign walks E1 legally, persists each leg, and hashes deterministically”). Scope item 5 is deliberately **resolve-and-REPORT**: whether `--resume` can supply an E3-active profile is answered in writing, because forcing a locked contract to run is a sim-semantics call with canon reach and is not a fire’s to take. Lane currency verified per F-1320-2: `lane/a` contains `9849623b6` and its copy of the harness is **byte-identical to main**. 💡 **THE REUSABLE HALF, AND IT IS A SHARPENING OF F-2086-2 RATHER THAN A REPEAT: when a finding names a class of denominators, sweeping ONE member is not sweeping the class — and the row that records the partial sweep will read like a completed one.** F-2086-2’s closing sentence is correct and was carried forward faithfully as prose by every fire since; what none of them did was **execute it against the second denominator**. ⚠️ **A `RECORDED-NOT-BUILT` clause is the highest-value thing on the board for an idle fire and the easiest to miss, because it looks like a finished finding: it is a 🔺 row, it has a hash, it is drained, and its work is entirely undone.** Grep the findings rows for `RECORDED-NOT-BUILT` and `GATE:` before ever writing PIPELINE-DRY again. ✅ **SHIPPED s2122 `ce75bc31970404a9a48c8e94a70c542739739112`** — drained by the s2122 fire; review `reviews/f2120-1-campaign-harness-contract-select.md`, leaf `f2120-1-campaign-harness-contract-select` → `merged`. **The runner delivered all six scope items and added a fourth refusal arm that the master's own ⚠️ clause required but its numbered list omitted** (`practice.scores === false`, which would otherwise have filtered to an empty board and exited 0 having measured nothing); its independent `codex review` also caught `--contract=` falling through to the default board, now pinned by a sixth test. Gates on the merged tree in a detached `gate-s2122` worktree (§3.0b custody): tsc rc=0 · build rc=0 · own spec **6/6** · `fixture-teardown` **34 subjects** rc=0 · `function-cors-allowlist` 2/2 · `gate-caller-audit` 26/26 · all three named refusal arms **reproduced verbatim by the drain's own control run**, exit 1 each. ⚠️ **Two findings filed, both non-blocking and both worth reading before the next E3 slice.** **F-2122-1**: the merge **rotted a `src/**` line citation** — `src/sim/HeadlessContractSim.ts:715` cited `gr-sim-campaign.mjs:88` for the profile-storage write site, which the slice's +15 lines moved to `:103`, leaving `:88` on an unrelated `if (!contract) break;`. Re-based in the landing commit `11256af3c` per the `CLAUDE.md` §4.10b lifecycle and re-phrased to name the CODE at the site. **The class is the point: `law-pointer-guard` watches law surfaces only, so a `src/**` comment citing a `scripts/**` line rots SILENTLY and forever.** No guard proposed — one over every backticked `file:line` in `src/` comments would red constantly and earn a permanent excuse (the `cross-engine` fate, F-1460-1). **F-2122-2**: the **full `test:node-guards` battery did NOT run on the merged tree**, and the reason is machine arrangement, not judgement — a concurrent attended six-file battery (pid 40448) plus a five-way playwright chromium fleet took 1-min load from **18 → 78 → 362** and held it at **241–362**. Two disqualifying effects: `node-guards-contention` reds by design on a detected second battery (a **false red about my own drain** — the same predeclared red the runner hit in-lane), and F-2076-1 measured per-test budget margins moving **2.0–2.4× with load**. I ran the **measured dependency surface** instead — `grep -rln gr-sim-campaign` returns exactly five hits, of which the only code dependents are the own test and `function-cors-allowlist` (both green), plus `fixture-teardown` because the diff adds a new `mkdtemp` prefix. ➡️ **OWED, one command on a quiet machine: `node scripts/run-guards.mjs --changed-sinc