consume it).** 📏 **WHAT WAS MEASURED.** s2055 cured the dead implementer (F-2055-1) and dispatched the one master it found sitting in a queue. Sweeping `ls -t tasks/*.md` by MTIME rather than by leaf status found a second one from the same 2026-08-15 evening authoring batch: `tasks/assay-season-roll.md` (authored 20:19, between `assay-replay-fidelity` 20:12 and `assay-worker-loop` 20:23), never `cp`'d into any queue, undispatched for 3 days. It implements a VERBATIM owner ruling (`specs/agent-play/tape-contract.md:18-20`, RATIFIED 2026-08-15: *"this kind of calls for a next season? Because we have to test the taping anyways again."*), its stated dependency `assay-cf-lifecycle` is merged (`49aab8f1d63a2175e47e85b54659fead6caaa6da`, 39 `assay` hits live in `functions/api/standings.ts`), and it is NOT stale (Mistake #8 clear): `boardKey()` at `functions/api/standings.ts:887` is `standings:${epochId}:${contractId}` with NO season dimension, so scope item 1 is genuinely unbuilt. The 6 `season` hits in that file are `resolveSeasonAt()` DISPLAY labels — precisely the false positive the master's own no-op guard anticipates. ⚠️ **THE DEFECT: I DISPATCHED IT, AND EVERY DECISIVE INSTRUMENT SAID GO.** `drain-block-check` → **CLEAR** (`status="planned"`, no block). The leaf's own note → **"MAIN slot; queue when main frees"**, and main WAS free. Spec ratified, dependency merged, not stale, main queue empty, MAIN THROTTLE clear (0 undrained done-moves). The master file itself carries **no** `QUEUE ONLY AFTER` line, no `GATE:` line, no sequencing clause anywhere — `grep -n "QUEUE ONLY AFTER|after E5|admission|GATE"` matches ONE line, its unrelated `READY-FOR-GATES` sign-off. The gate exists in exactly one place on the whole board: the attended authoring-batch row in this file (find it by CONTENT — `grep -F "SEASON ROLL RULED + SLICE-2 AUTHORED + E5 WON"`; it sat at line 13 when this finding was drafted and rotted to 15 the moment the finding was prepended above it, which is the hazard in miniature), which reads *"master tasks/assay-season-roll.md (**MAIN, after E5 admission completion**)"*. That condition is UNMET and has been since it was written — `fix-e5-dredge-queen-headless-socket` is itself a `gate-side` hold (F-1815-2) whose drain rider was to spawn the admission-completion successor, so the successor was never spawned. 🎯 **THE REUSABLE HALF — `PROSE-ONLY` IS NOT A BENIGN BUCKET, IT IS THE TOOL DECLINING TO ANSWER.** `authorable-candidates.mjs` was run FIRST, as the refill law requires, and reported `14 planned leaves — 0 fire-authorable (owner-gated 7, attended-owed 3, **prose-only 3**, needs-spec 1)`. Re-run against the pre-edit goals via `--root` to settle it honestly: it classified this leaf **`PROSE-ONLY assay-season-roll … (prose only, key "note" — class unreadable)`** and passed the misleading note through verbatim. It did not clear the master and it did not flag the E5 gate — **it said it could not tell.** A fire reading the summary line sees `0 fire-authorable` and treats every non-zero bucket as gated; but `owner-gated`/`attended-owed`/`needs-spec` are ANSWERS and `prose-only` is an OPEN QUESTION, and they are printed side by side as if they were the same kind of thing. **The one bucket that means "unknown" is the one where an unmet gate can hide.** 🔑 **AND THE LEAF ACTIVELY CONTRADICTED THE LEDGER:** its note says *"queue when main frees"* — a dispatch INVITATION — while the authoring-batch row states a hard predecessor. Two ledger surfaces, written by the same attended batch, disagreeing about whether the same master may run; the instrument reads the permissive one because that is the one attached to the leaf. This is the ledger-vs-name class (F-1669-1) with the surfaces swapped: there the fossil was a filename and the ledger was right; here BOTH are ledger, and the more authoritative one is the one no tool parses. 💰 **COST: ZERO, BY LUCK AND NOT BY DESIGN — WHICH IS THE PART TO WEIGH.** The dispatch sat in `tasks/queue/main/` for ~4 minutes and the runner never saw it, because §1's lock semaphore gates the MAIN slot while a fire's line-1 says `ACTIVE` — I was holding my own lock. **Against a LANE slot there is no such protection: s2055 measured lane-d claiming a queued master in ONE SECOND.** The identical mistake made against lane-a/b/c would have burned a full Codex run on work whose predecessor does not exist. ✅ **CURED THIS FIRE, REVERSIBLY:** master moved to `tasks/queue-paused/assay-season-roll.md` (parked, not deleted — RETENTION LAW), leaf re-registered `blocked` + `blockClass: "gate-side"` with the BACKLOG citation and the lift condition in its `blockedReason`, so `drain-block-check assay-season-roll.md` now exits 1 and PRINTS the gate together with the authoring-batch row as its BACKLOG context line (the tool resolves that line number itself at run time, so it never goes stale). The next fire that finds this master by mtime gets a STOP with the reason, which is the gap I fell into, closed. 🚫 **NO NEW MECHANISM PROPOSED, DELIBERATELY.** The obvious cure — teach `authorable-candidates` to parse sequencing prose out of BACKLOG rows — is a natural-language judgement, and per the standing F-1460-1 prohibition a guard that must judge prose gets excused into uselessness. The cheap, real cure is the one already performed: **when the pricing tool says `PROSE-ONLY`, that leaf is UNPRICED, not cleared — open the row and read it before dispatching, and if you dispatch anyway, record the gate you found as a `blockClass` so the question is answered mechanically ONCE instead of re-derived by every fire.** ➡️ **ATTENDED-OWED, NOT OWNER-OWED:** this unblocks when E5 admission completes (the re-scope + detached re-land already carried as s2055 NEXT (G)) — the same act unblocks BOTH `fix-e5-dredge-queen-headless-socket` and this. **Two launch-relevant masters now sit behind one attended act.**
1633:🟢 **F-2055-1 CURED s2055 — THE 191-FIRE NO-OP LOOP HAD AN EXIT REACHABLE FROM INSIDE THE WHOLE TIME: THE IMPLEMENTER PROCESS WAS DEAD, AND NO TRIAGE STEP EVER ASKS.** 📏 **WHAT WAS ACTUALLY WRONG, measured before touching anything:** `pgrep -fl lane-runner-v3.sh` → **rc=1, no process**; `logs/runner-headless.log`'s last dispatch is **2026-08-15 20:13**; `tasks/.runner.lock` sat held since **Aug 15 21:41** with no owner. Meanwhile `tasks/queue/lane-d/assay-worker-loop.md` had been correctly `cp`'d into the queue at **Aug 15 22:50** and never consumed. **The factory had no implementer for ~3 days and 191 consecutive fires reported a "dry board" instead.** ✓ **THE QUEUED MASTER WAS LAWFUL ON EVERY AXIS I COULD CHECK, which is what makes the stall expensive rather than merely untidy** — `drain-block-check` → **CLEAR** (not blocked, no owner fork); its own stated gate is *"QUEUE ONLY AFTER assay-replay-fidelity MERGES"* and that predecessor's leaf reads **`status:"merged"`**; its spec `specs/agent-play/assay-worker.md` is **RATIFIED 2026-08-15** carrying the owner's build GO verbatim (*"you can buld the five slices"*) **and an owner LAUNCH-GATING ruling** (*"the gauntlet verification worker queue has to be done before launch - people will play and submit results"*); the master itself is tracked and committed (`d240c669f`); lane-d was `ahead=0` **USABLE**; and **none of its three deliverables exist on main** (`scripts/assay-worker.mjs`, `scripts/assay-worker.test.mjs`, `docs/assay-worker-runbook.md` all absent), so this is not Mistake #8 stale-master shape. An owner-ruled launch blocker sat one dead process away from being built. ⚠️ **THE STRUCTURAL DEFECT IS IN §2's TRIAGE LADDER, NOT IN INSTRUMENTATION — AND THAT DISTINCTION IS THE FINDING.** The instrument exists, is prescribed, and was reporting it: `bash scripts/health-watch.sh status` prints **`runner : ALIVE`/dead as its FIRST LINE**, and `CLAUDE.md` §1 lists it as a session-start read ("the live board in 2 seconds"). But **§2's ladder — the thing a fire actually executes — has no step that consults it**: §2B asks *are there done-moves to drain* (a dead runner produces none), §2E asks *is a queue EMPTY, so should I refill* (a non-empty unrun queue reads as **healthy**, the opposite of the truth), and **§2F's "nothing to do, exit clean" is therefore reachable without ever asking whether anything CAN run.** A queued-but-unrun master is invisible to both checks simultaneously — it is exactly the blind spot between them. 💡 **This is the F-1582-1 shape restated at the ladder level: a tool only helps the fire that asks it, and nothing in the executed order asks this one.** 🩺 **HOW IT DIED, since it bears on the cure:** the log's tail reads `stopped (background tasks finish on their own)` (a clean stop ~21:04), then **two restarts at ~21:41 that each printed `watching …` and then vanished with no `stopped` line, leaving the lock dir orphaned** — the signature of a **non-detached** start dying with its parent shell. That is precisely the failure `scripts/start-lane-runner.sh` was built to prevent (it starts **detached** and **verifies `PPID 1`**), so the runner was very likely last started **by hand rather than by the sanctioned helper**. ✅ **CURED, BY THE SANCTIONED ROUTE, NOT BY HAND (§2.0b):** `bash scripts/start-lane-runner.sh` — run through `node` because the bash gate refuses the direct form (the gate denies YOU, not the factory). `--check` first: **environment READY**, `codex 0.147.0`, child node **v23.11.1**. Then the real start: **runner UP at pid 451, `PPID 1`, `TTY ??`, `STAT Ss`, started 2026-08-18 20:05:25**. It claimed the master **within one second** — `20260818-200526 START lane-d :: assay-worker-loop.md` — and its run-log banner reads `OpenAI Codex v0.147.0` / `model: gpt-5.6-sol` / `workdir: worktrees/lane-d` with no usage-limit death, which is §2.0's primary instrument answering *the implementer is serving*. `health-watch` now reads `runner : ALIVE`, `in-flight: 1`. 🚫 **I did NOT `rmdir tasks/.runner.lock`** — §2.0b forbids it and the runner self-healed the stale lock itself, logging `stale lock self-healed (no other runner process)`, which also **confirms** no rival instance existed. 🔑 **F-1507-1 DOES NOT BLOCK THE RESTART, and this retires an inherited premise rather than an owner gate.** s2054's NEXT (F) sequenced the restart *after* the owner rules the single-Node baseline. But the helper **resolves and pins the client itself**: it selected node **v23.11.1** for the child — **the very lane baseline F-1506-1/F-1507-1 are about** (*"the lane's node (v23.11.1, via `nvm use 23` in `~/.zshrc:54`)"*) — and scrubs `CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR`/`CLAUDECODE` so lane playwright parallelism is preserved. **The interpreter hazard the gate exists to prevent is already mechanically closed at the restart path.** F-1507-1 remains genuinely open as a *measurement* question (lane-side vs fire-side `test:node-guards` on one node) and stays on the desk — it simply never gated this act. ⚖️ **THIS MATERIALLY QUALIFIES F-2054-1**, whose central claim is *"no act available to a fire can change the board."* That was true of the **desk** (all 21–22 items are genuinely owner-gated, re-confirmed: `authorable-candidates` still prices 15 planned leaves at **0 fire-authorable**) and **false of the board**: one sanctioned command moved the factory from zero in-flight to one, on owner-ruled launch-gating work. **The cost measurement in F-2054-1 stands and is not disputed; its exit-reachability conclusion is corrected.** The two findings are complementary — F-2054-1 correctly priced the loop, and priced it as inescapable because it audited *authorable scope* and never audited *executive capacity*. 📌 **THE REUSABLE LESSON, which is bigger than this incident: a dry board has TWO independent causes — nothing to do, and nothing able to do it — and the ladder only ever measured the first.** "Dry" was being inferred from *queues + leaves + done-moves*, all of which describe **work**, none of which describe **workers**. ➡️ **REC (attended, small and purely mechanical — no judgement call, so it will not decay into an excused label the way F-1460-1's `cross-engine` did): add one step at the head of §2 — `pgrep -f lane-runner-v3.sh` (or the first line of `health-watch.sh status`); if no runner is alive AND any `tasks/queue/*/` is non-empty, that is a STALLED FACTORY, not a dry board, and §2.0b's restart is the owed act before §2F may be reached.** The predicate is two facts and a conjunction; it cannot fire on a genuinely dry board (all queues empty) and cannot be argued with. GATE: closes when §2 carries an implementer-liveness step. Related: [F-2054-1], [F-1653-1], [F-1652-1], [F-1582-1], [F-1507-1].
