1769:🧪 **F-1679-1 (s1679 2026-08-11, MEASURED ON THE ELEVENTH CONSECUTIVE HELD FIRE — I RAN A SECOND PROBE TO CONFIRM A FIRST, AND IT CONFIRMED IT. BOTH WERE WRONG, FOR TWO UNRELATED MECHANICAL REASONS, AND THE DISCONFIRMING CONTROL WAS ALREADY PRINTED ON MY OWN SCREEN. NOT AN OWNER QUESTION.)** 📋 **THE NEAR-MISS, stated first because the finding is about how close it came:** while pre-proving the banked `f1662-2` dispatch I asked whether its Step 1 refresh target exists, ran `git rev-parse --short main origin/main`, and got **rc=128 `fatal: Needed a single revision`** with **no output at all**. Read while asking *does this ref exist?*, that text says **no**. 🔁 **I THEN DID THE RIGHT-LOOKING THING AND CHECKED IT A SECOND WAY** — `git for-each-ref --format='%(refname) %(objectname:short)' refs/remotes/origin/ | grep -E "origin/(main|master|HEAD)$"` → **0 hits**. Two independent commands, same answer. The finding I was one commit from filing: *"`origin/main` does not exist, so `f1662-2`'s Step 1 refresh target is missing and the dispatch would fail"* — dramatic, actionable, and **completely false**; it would have frozen a ready dispatch behind a phantom infrastructure defect. ✓ **BOTH MECHANISMS MEASURED, NOT GUESSED.** (1) **`--short` TAKES ONE REVISION.** `git rev-parse main origin/main` (same two revs, no `--short`) prints **both** hashes fine: `d19a5c1fd30b…` and `f8a6af221d16…`. The message is about the **ARITY OF MY ARGUMENT LIST**, not about any revision failing to resolve — an error text that **accuses the wrong subject**, the same shape as the false *"lane is stale"* STOP that F-1424-3/F-1668-1 were filed for. (2) **`$` CANNOT ANCHOR AGAINST A MULTI-FIELD `--format`.** Each line read `refs/remotes/origin/HEAD f8a6af221`, so the anchor sat before a space, not at end-of-line. Same pattern, one-field format: `git for-each-ref --format='%(refname)' … | grep -cE "origin/(main|master|HEAD)$"` → **2**; two-field → **0**. **The `--format` field count silently decides whether an anchored grep can ever match.** 🎯 **THE REUSABLE HALF, AND IT IS THE THIRD IN A FAMILY THIS DOCUMENT HAS BEEN WRITING ALL DAY — F-1676-1: *control a ZERO before believing it*; F-1678-1: *control a HIT before believing it condemns*; THIS ONE: A SECOND PROBE WRITTEN TO CONFIRM A FIRST INHERITS THE FIRST'S CONCLUSION, SO ITS AGREEMENT IS NOT CORROBORATION.** The two failures here were **mechanically independent** (an arity rule; an anchor rule) yet **epistemically correlated**, because I authored the second one already believing the answer and accepted it the moment it matched. Independence of mechanism buys nothing when the aiming is shared. ➡️ **THEREFORE, BINDING FOR ANY CONFIRMING PROBE: it must be one that COULD have disconfirmed — and the cheapest sufficient version is to make it report a KNOWN-PRESENT CONTROL in the same command.** ⚠️ **HERE THAT CONTROL COST NOTHING AND WAS ALREADY IN HAND: `refs/remotes/origin/HEAD` had been printed by my OWN previous command, twenty seconds earlier, and the confirming grep dropped it too.** A ref I had just seen with my eyes returning **0** was the disconfirmation, sitting in the scrollback — I did not compare, because I was reading the output for the answer I expected rather than for the answer I already knew. **When a probe returns the answer you predicted, check what it says about something you can already see.** ✅ **OPERATIONAL RESULT ONCE THE PROBES WERE CORRECTED — `f1662-2` IS DISPATCH-READY AND PRE-PROVED, WHICH IS THE HELD BUDGET'S DELIVERABLE:** `origin/main` resolves to **`f8a6af221`**, local `main` is **1 ahead** (this fire's own lock commit), and **all five pre-flight keys PASS at BOTH refs** — checked at `origin/main` specifically because that, not local `main`, is what the master's Step 1 refreshes onto. Keys 1–4 read exactly **1** (`scripts/same-game-audit.mjs` ×3; `src/sim/HeadlessContractSim.ts:62`), key 5 reads **2**, satisfying `>= 1` exactly as **F-1668-1** predicted it would drift. ⓘ **This row deliberately does NOT quote key 5's needle verbatim**, so that reporting the count does not increment it — F-1668-1's own trap, avoided by construction rather than by luck. 🔬 **AND THE DEFECT THE MASTER CURES IS STILL LIVE, RE-REPRODUCED HERE RATHER THAN INHERITED (its item 0, done for free before dispatch):** `CONTRACT_ADMISSION_EXEMPTIONS` holds **8** entries (`e2-hill-mine`, `e2-incline`, `e2-trestle`, `e3-fairground`, `e5-deepwater-claim`, `e5-stillwater`, `e6-glow-mesa`, `e6-showroom`), while `docs/bench/same-game-audit.md:27` still ends *"leaving five cited exemptions"* with its own **8**-row `### Cited exemptions` table at `:44–:53` directly beneath. **Zero coordinate rot** against the s1663 master sixteen fires later: prose still `:27`, rows still `:46–:53`, all three F-E2S-3 reasons still byte-identical. **Nobody cured it, nothing moved, the master's STOP conditions are all clear — the next fire dispatches rather than re-verifies.**
1781:🔧 **F-1666-2 (s1666 2026-08-11, MEASURED + CURED THE SAME FIRE — THE DESK-CARRYFORWARD GUARD IS FULLY BUILT, CORRECT, TEETH-PROVEN, GRANDFATHERED AS "CALLED BY A LAW" — AND NOTHING HAS EVER RUN IT AGAINST THE LIVE BOARD. ITS FIRST MEASURED CASUALTY IS THE FIRE IMMEDIATELY BEFORE ME. NOT AN OWNER QUESTION.)** 📋 **HOW IT SURFACED, which matters because nobody was looking for it:** §4 tells a fire to run `desk-state-audit --status` on what it INHERITED. I did. It returned **1 item** (`F-1659-1`, OPEN-DESK-ONLY) — while s1665's own line-1 header declares **"OWNER'S DESK — 21 awaiting a word"** and visibly lists 21 ids. **A 20-item discrepancy between a desk's own header and the instrument that reads it.** 🎯 **ROOT CAUSE — AN ENCODING CHANGE, NOT A DROP.** `deskItems()` (`desk-carryforward-guard.mjs:152`) splits the desk tail on **🔺** and takes the first id in each segment's `KEY_ZONE`; the convention is therefore **one 🔺 per item**. **s1664's line-1 carries 22 markers → parses as 21 items ✓. s1665's carries 2** — one header, then all 21 ids run together under a single 🔺 separated by `·` — **so it parses as 1.** s1665 did **not** drop anything and its count of 21 was honestly obtained (it ran the audit on s1664's line and reported `OPEN-DESK-ONLY=21` faithfully); **it wrote a correct desk in a format its own reader cannot see.** ⚠️ **THE CONSEQUENCE IS THE SILENT-DROP CLASS, ONE FIRE FROM HAPPENING:** the next fire doing exactly what §4 orders reads **one** item and carries **one** forward, retiring twenty owner-desk items that no owner ever ruled on — including `F-E2S-3`, `F-DOOR-6` and `F-1101-1`. That is precisely the s1527 event (`ten dropped, incl. "13 of 25 campaign maps cannot be opened"`) that `desk-carryforward-guard` was BUILT to prevent. 🔍 **AND THE GUARD CATCHES IT PERFECTLY — I PROVED IT BY MANUFACTURING, NOT BY A GREEN (the s1299/s1300 standard).** Reconstructed s1665's exact post-handoff `STATUS.md` in `/tmp/s1666-deskrepro` (its handoff as line-1, its own archive bullet removed) and ran the guard: **rc=1**, `previous desk (s1664): 21 items · this desk: 1 items · dropped: 20 (0 accounted for)`, naming all twenty. **The instrument was never the problem.** ⛔ **THE ACTUAL DEFECT: NOTHING INVOKES IT. MEASURED THREE WAYS.** **(1)** `test:ledger-guards` names `scripts/desk-carryforward-guard.test.mjs` — the **unit test**, which drives the guard against *fixture* roots (`desk-carryforward-guard.test.mjs:34` passes `--root <fixture>`), never against live `STATUS.md`. **(2)** `grep -c "desk-carryforward" scripts/fire.md` → **0**. **(3)** Its `gate-caller-baseline.json:12` grandfather entry excuses it from the reachability audit with *"Its caller is a LAW (scripts/fire.md §4, run as the last act)"* — **and that law has never named it.** 💡 **THE REUSABLE HALF, AND IT IS ABOUT THE EXCUSE, NOT THE GUARD: a grandfather entry is an ASSERTION ABOUT A CALLER, and nothing checks that the asserted caller exists.** The entry is otherwise impeccable — it cites its finding, explains the structural timing, and records teeth proven by manufacturing. Its one unverified clause is the one load-bearing for coverage. ⓘ **Its siblings are genuinely fine and that is the discriminator I used rather than assuming a class-wide rot:** `status-archive-audit.mjs` and `attended-owed-audit.mjs` make the *identical* law claim, but `test:ledger-guards` **also calls each of them live** (`node scripts/status-archive-audit.mjs --limit 40 --quiet && node scripts/attended-owed-audit.mjs`), so they run every fire regardless. `desk-carryforward-guard.mjs` was the **only** post-bookkeeping guard resting on the law claim ALONE. ➡️ **CURED IN ONE LINE, IN THE BATTERY ITS OWN BASELINE ARGUES FOR:** `test:ledger-guards` now runs `node scripts/desk-carryforward-guard.mjs` live, beside `test:desk-declaration` and `test:desk-birth` — the two desk guards already wired exactly this way. **This is the placement the baseline entry itself endorses** ("must stay post-bookkeeping rather than move into the pre-merge battery"), so it contradicts nothing: the guard **SKIPs on an `ACTIVE` lock line**, so it is a no-op mid-fire and bites only after the handoff commit, which is when the desk exists. ✅ `gate-caller-audit` re-run after the change: **PASS** (reachability is computed from ROOTS and `test:ledger-guards` is deliberately unrooted, so the grandfather entry stays correct and stays needed). 🩹 **THE INHERITED DESK IS REPAIRED IN THIS FIRE'S OWN HANDOFF, not merely reported:** all **21** items are carried forward with **one 🔺 each**, restoring the format the parser reads. The true set is not my reconstruction — it is `desk-state-audit --status` run against **s1664's** archived line-1, which returns `CLOSED=0 · OPEN=0 · BOTH=0 · OPEN-DESK-ONLY=21 · UNRECORDED=0`, i.e. **nothing among the 21 has silently closed and none is a ghost.** ⓘ **No new mechanism is proposed for the `·`-run encoding itself** — the count refusal already catches it (`declared 21` vs `keyed 1`), and it went unseen only because the guard holding that check was never run. **Wiring the existing guard is the whole cure.**
1784:✅ **F-1660-1 (s1660 2026-08-11, MEASURED ON A BOARD THAT REPORTED ITSELF DRY FOR THE FOURTH CONSECUTIVE FIRE — AN OWNER RULING, MERGED AND GATED TWO DAYS AGO, HAS BEEN SILENTLY REVERSED ON MAIN FOR ~15 HOURS, AND THE GUARD THAT WATCHES THAT SURFACE STAYED GREEN THROUGHOUT BECAUSE AGREEMENT IS EXACTLY WHAT IT ASSERTS.)** 🧭 **HOW I FOUND IT:** the board was dry and s1659's NEXT(B) pointed at the `planned` leaves, so I priced them per §2E — and checked the owner's own 2026-08-09 rulings roundup rather than the leaf titles, per Mistake #4. Item 2 of that roundup reads *"F-E2S-3 RULED: de-list now, socket later … FIRE-AUTHORABLE, two masters, the de-list first"*, and the row two lines below it (F-1608-2, an OWNER'S DESK item) states as settled fact *"Both maps are currently door-de-listed per F-E2S-3, so nothing is blocked meanwhile."* ✗ **BOTH ARE FALSE ON MAIN TODAY, AND I PROVED IT WITH THE PRODUCT RATHER THAN A LEDGER:** `node scripts/gr-sim.mjs --contract=e2-hill-mine --seed=e2-hill-mine-01 --policy=idle` **runs a full contract and emits a view — no mode supplied, no throw.** `public/skill.md`'s public door fence lists `e2-hill-mine`, `e2-incline` and `e2-trestle` again. 📜 **THE CHAIN, EACH LINK VERIFIED BY READING THE COMMIT, NOT THE ROW:** the ruling shipped as `f1605-1` at **`88530e3ef`** (authored s1605, drained s1606, review `reviews/f1605-1-e2s3-door-delist.md`), which de-listed the three from `SUPPORTED_CONTRACTS` and **deliberately left the escort-mode arm intact** so escort callers were untouched. Then **`48a0d41ab`** (the `ap16-4-same-game-admission` runner commit, 2026-08-11T00:02, `git merge-base --is-ancestor` → on main) replaced the hand-maintained literal with a derivation — registry minus a new `CONTRACT_ADMISSION_EXEMPTIONS` table. **The four E5/E6 refusals and `e3-fairground` were carried across as cited entries; the three E2 railcars were not**, so the derivation swept them back in. 🔑 **THE ROOT IS ONE SENTENCE OF ARITHMETIC IN THE AP-16-4 MA