sm to act — it needs this row. **GATE: none for the 9 (any fire may take them, one per fire); an attended nod for the `gateClass` column; the 19 stay owner-gated and unchanged. Reverse the whole row with one word — nothing was deleted, F-1589-3 and F-1588-1 keep their BACKLOG rows and only leave the DESK.** 📌 **CONVENTION, ADDED s1604 PER F-1603-1 — WHEN YOU TAKE ONE OF THE 9, FLIP THE ROW HEAD IN THE SAME COMMIT AS THE CURE: A TAIL-APPENDED `CURED` IS NOT A CLOSURE.** The closure detectors (`subjectLedClosure`, and `findings-state-guard`'s `scan` beneath it) read the row HEAD, not its body, while the house cure convention appends `✅ **CURED sNNNN` to the TAIL of a long row whose head keeps its open marker — so the cure is invisible to the detector. **Three consecutive work-act cures were invisible this way: s1601 (F-1587-1), s1602 (F-1589-2), s1603 (F-1588-2)** — but ⚖️ **MEASURED s1604 BY REPLAYING `desk-carryforward-guard` AGAINST ALL THREE FIRES' OWN HANDOFFS, ONLY ONE ACTUALLY TRIPPED IT, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS THE USEFUL PART.** s1602: `dropped:[F-1589-2] · silent:[F-1589-2]`, **rc=1 — a handoff its own last-act battery would have REFUSED**; s1601: dropped 3, `silent:[]`, saved because its line-1 ACKNOWLEDGED each drop in prose; s1603: `silent:[]`, saved because it flipped its own head. ➡️ **So there are two ways to stay green and only one is durable:** an acknowledgement clause saves the FIRE that writes it and nothing after, while a flipped head makes the closure readable to every reader forever. Write the head flip; the acknowledgement is not a substitute. ✓ **DISCHARGED s1604 for all three** — F-1588-2 flipped by s1603, and F-1587-1 (`22ff19c1d`) + F-1589-2 (`c889c0539`) flipped this fire after confirming each cure landed **in the code, not the prose**: the live instrument prints both behaviours (`! CORRECTION … OUTRANKS THE SNAPSHOT BELOW` above the stale bucket; `! STALE SNAPSHOT — 12 days old, threshold 7` per-outcome between verdict and body), `red-inve